# Titanic - Exploratory Data Analysis 

## FIRST(DATA SET)
import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import seaborn as sns

df = pd.read_csv('https://raw.githubusercontent.com/datasciencedojo/datasets/master/titanic.csv')
df.head()
## SECOND ( I WILL ASK 4 QUESTIONS WITH HYPOTHESES BEFORE THAT AND AFTER THAT CHECK FOR RESULTS)
FIRST QUESTION : IS THE MOST SURVIVORS FEMALES OR MALES ?
logically females is the most survivors because of the history cause (Women and Children First Policy) .
CODE : 
sns.countplot(data=df, x='Survived', hue='Sex')
plt.title('Survival Count by Sex')
plt.show() .
RESULT : results show a significant majority of women among the survivors .
SECOND QUESTION : IS THE MONEY BOUGHT THE LIFE THAT NIGHT ?
there are a diffirence between classes in the ship , the protect is better in high classe and this is a cause can survive peoples .
CODE :
sns.barplot(data=df, x='Pclass', y='Survived')
plt.title('Survival Rate by Ticket Class')
plt.show()
RESULT : The higher the section, the higher the survival rate.
THIRD QUESTION : What is the age distribution of the survivors? 
In reality, there is no logical reason why age difference would make a difference in such an incident, as survival depends on the situation and location.
CODE : 
sns.histplot(data=df, x='Age', hue='Survived', multiple='stack', kde=True)
plt.title('Age Distribution of Survived vs Deceased')
plt.show()
RESULT : The results indicate that the closer the age is to that of a young adult, the higher the survival rate, contrary to the hypothesis.
LAST QUESTION : Was having family a saving grace or an obstacle?
Logically, unity among family members and facing difficult situations in an alliance will certainly be easier, and this leads to a greater likelihood of families surviving.
CODE : 
df['FamilySize'] = df['SibSp'] + df['Parch']
sns.lineplot(data=df, x='FamilySize', y='Survived')
plt.title('Survival Rate by Family Size')
plt.show()
RESULT : 
"The analysis shows that family size was a crucial factor in survival, but inversely so beyond a certain threshold. Small families (1 to 3 people) had the best chance of survival, with a rate exceeding 70%. In contrast, very large families (more than 4 people) and solo travelers had great difficulty surviving, with the probability dropping sharply, indicating that the chaotic evacuation of the ship made rescuing large groups almost impossible."
